    question: "What is HTML fundamentals?",
    slug: "html-css-what-is-html-fundamentals-1",
    shortDescription: "Frequently asked HTML-CSS interview question about HTML fundamentals.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nHTML structures web documents with elements and attributes. Browsers parse that markup into a DOM that scripts and styles can work with.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define HTML fundamentals accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: HTML structures web documents with elements and attributes. Browsers parse that markup into a DOM that scripts and styles can work with.\n2. Use a concrete case: A product page might use headings, a navigation element, a form, and buttons so the browser has a meaningful document structure.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: HTML gives structure rather than visual design; good semantics can improve accessibility and maintainability.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define HTML fundamentals first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA product page might use headings, a navigation element, a form, and buttons so the browser has a meaningful document structure.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA web application may use HTML fundamentals when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a product page might use headings, a navigation element, a form, and buttons so the browser has a meaningful document structure. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating HTML fundamentals as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with HTML fundamentals.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor HTML fundamentals, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nHTML structures web documents with elements and attributes. Browsers parse that markup into a DOM that scripts and styles can work with.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define HTML fundamentals accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: HTML structures web documents with elements and attributes. Browsers parse that markup into a DOM that scripts and styles can work with.\n2. Use a concrete case: A product page might use headings, a navigation element, a form, and buttons so the browser has a meaningful document structure.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: HTML gives structure rather than visual design; good semantics can improve accessibility and maintainability.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define HTML fundamentals first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA product page might use headings, a navigation element, a form, and buttons so the browser has a meaningful document structure.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA web application may use HTML fundamentals when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a product page might use headings, a navigation element, a form, and buttons so the browser has a meaningful document structure. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating HTML fundamentals as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with HTML fundamentals.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor HTML fundamentals, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
    explanation: "What is HTML fundamentals? is a core HTML-CSS interview concept. A strong answer should define the concept clearly, explain how it works, show where it is useful, and discuss practical trade-offs. The candidate should connect the explanation to a realistic engineering or business scenario and distinguish the concept from closely related alternatives.",
    keyPoints: ["Core definition of the concept", "How it works in HTML-CSS", "Practical use case or example", "Benefits and limitations", "Important trade-offs and alternatives"],
    commonMistakes: ["Giving only a memorized definition without explaining practical usage", "Ignoring important limitations or trade-offs", "Confusing the concept with a related HTML-CSS concept", "Using an example that does not match the stated requirement", "Failing to discuss how the approach would be validated in practice"],
    followUpQuestions: ["Why is HTML fundamentals important?", "How does HTML fundamentals work?", "What are the main benefits of HTML fundamentals?", "What are the common mistakes with HTML fundamentals?", "How would you use HTML fundamentals in a real project?"],
