    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nHTML fundamentals is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. HTML gives structure rather than visual design; good semantics can improve accessibility and maintainability. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what HTML fundamentals gives you: HTML structures web documents with elements and attributes. Browsers parse that markup into a DOM that scripts and styles can work with.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: HTML gives structure rather than visual design; good semantics can improve accessibility and maintainability.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: A product page might use headings, a navigation element, a form, and buttons so the browser has a meaningful document structure.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nA product page might use headings, a navigation element, a form, and buttons so the browser has a meaningful document structure.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA web application may use HTML fundamentals when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a product page might use headings, a navigation element, a form, and buttons so the browser has a meaningful document structure. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating HTML fundamentals as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with HTML fundamentals.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
