    shortDescription: "Frequently asked HTML-CSS interview question about Semantic HTML.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nIt matters because semantic HTML uses elements according to their meaning, such as `main`, `nav`, `article`, `button`, and `form`, instead of treating every part of a page as a generic container. In practice, the example shows how that behavior is used: use `<button>` for an action rather than a clickable `<div>` so keyboard and assistive-technology behavior comes from the platform.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe question is looking for reasoning rather than another definition. Explain the problem the concept solves, then say what would make you choose a different approach.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State the problem or need.\n2. Explain how Semantic HTML addresses it: Semantic HTML uses elements according to their meaning, such as `main`, `nav`, `article`, `button`, and `form`, instead of treating every part of a page as a generic container.\n3. Tie that benefit to the concrete situation: Use `<button>` for an action rather than a clickable `<div>` so keyboard and assistive-technology behavior comes from the platform.\n4. State the condition that can make the approach a poor fit: Semantics reduce the need for custom behavior, but the right element still depends on the actual interaction.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nAnswer the “why” directly, then connect it to a real engineering constraint. I would give one concrete situation and close by saying what would make me choose another approach.\n\n**Example :**\nUse `<button>` for an action rather than a clickable `<div>` so keyboard and assistive-technology behavior comes from the platform.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA web application may use Semantic HTML when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, use `<button>` for an action rather than a clickable `<div>` so keyboard and assistive-technology behavior comes from the platform. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Semantic HTML as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Semantic HTML.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nDo not repeat the definition as your whole answer. Explain the problem, the practical value, and the condition that would change your decision.",
