    slug: "html-css-how-does-semantic-html-work-13",
    shortDescription: "Frequently asked HTML-CSS interview question about Semantic HTML.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe usual flow for Semantic HTML is to start with the inputs or conditions, apply the behavior described here, and verify the resulting state. Semantic HTML uses elements according to their meaning, such as `main`, `nav`, `article`, `button`, and `form`, instead of treating every part of a page as a generic container.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the mechanism and can explain a sequence, not just the end result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Establish the starting condition.\n2. Apply the mechanism: Semantic HTML uses elements according to their meaning, such as `main`, `nav`, `article`, `button`, and `form`, instead of treating every part of a page as a generic container.\n3. Verify the result using the concrete case: Use `<button>` for an action rather than a clickable `<div>` so keyboard and assistive-technology behavior comes from the platform.\n4. Watch the trade-off or failure condition: Semantics reduce the need for custom behavior, but the right element still depends on the actual interaction.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nWalk through the normal sequence in order. Explain what changes at each important step, then mention how you would verify the result and what you would inspect if the operation failed.\n\n**Example :**\nUse `<button>` for an action rather than a clickable `<div>` so keyboard and assistive-technology behavior comes from the platform.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA web application may use Semantic HTML when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, use `<button>` for an action rather than a clickable `<div>` so keyboard and assistive-technology behavior comes from the platform. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Semantic HTML as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Semantic HTML.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the steps. That is more useful than listing commands or buzzwords.",
