    shortDescription: "Frequently asked HTML-CSS interview question about Semantic HTML.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Semantic HTML misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Semantic HTML. Semantic HTML uses elements according to their meaning, such as `main`, `nav`, `article`, `button`, and `form`, instead of treating every part of a page as a generic container.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Semantic HTML: Semantic HTML uses elements according to their meaning, such as `main`, `nav`, `article`, `button`, and `form`, instead of treating every part of a page as a generic container.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: Use `<button>` for an action rather than a clickable `<div>` so keyboard and assistive-technology behavior comes from the platform.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: Semantics reduce the need for custom behavior, but the right element still depends on the actual interaction.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nUse `<button>` for an action rather than a clickable `<div>` so keyboard and assistive-technology behavior comes from the platform.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA web application may use Semantic HTML when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, use `<button>` for an action rather than a clickable `<div>` so keyboard and assistive-technology behavior comes from the platform. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Semantic HTML as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Semantic HTML.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
