    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nSemantic HTML is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. Semantics reduce the need for custom behavior, but the right element still depends on the actual interaction. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what Semantic HTML gives you: Semantic HTML uses elements according to their meaning, such as `main`, `nav`, `article`, `button`, and `form`, instead of treating every part of a page as a generic container.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: Semantics reduce the need for custom behavior, but the right element still depends on the actual interaction.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: Use `<button>` for an action rather than a clickable `<div>` so keyboard and assistive-technology behavior comes from the platform.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nUse `<button>` for an action rather than a clickable `<div>` so keyboard and assistive-technology behavior comes from the platform.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA web application may use Semantic HTML when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, use `<button>` for an action rather than a clickable `<div>` so keyboard and assistive-technology behavior comes from the platform. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Semantic HTML as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Semantic HTML.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
