    shortDescription: "Frequently asked HTML-CSS interview question about Semantic HTML.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nI would explain Semantic HTML in plain language first, then give the technical detail that distinguishes it from nearby concepts. The practical anchor is: Use `<button>` for an action rather than a clickable `<div>` so keyboard and assistive-technology behavior comes from the platform.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can communicate the concept clearly at an interview pace and adjust the depth when the interviewer asks a follow-up.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Give a one-sentence definition: Semantic HTML uses elements according to their meaning, such as `main`, `nav`, `article`, `button`, and `form`, instead of treating every part of a page as a generic container.\n2. Explain one important behavior or distinction.\n3. Use the example to make it concrete: Use `<button>` for an action rather than a clickable `<div>` so keyboard and assistive-technology behavior comes from the platform.\n4. Finish with the limitation: Semantics reduce the need for custom behavior, but the right element still depends on the actual interaction.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would keep the first answer concise, then wait for the interviewer to signal whether they want internals. If they go deeper, I would expand the mechanism and trade-offs rather than repeating the definition.\n\n**Example :**\nUse `<button>` for an action rather than a clickable `<div>` so keyboard and assistive-technology behavior comes from the platform.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA web application may use Semantic HTML when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, use `<button>` for an action rather than a clickable `<div>` so keyboard and assistive-technology behavior comes from the platform. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Semantic HTML as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Semantic HTML.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nPractice saying {c} aloud in your own words. A technically correct answer is weaker if you cannot explain it without reading a script.",
