    shortDescription: "Frequently asked HTML-CSS interview question about HTML forms.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe main value of HTML forms comes from the behavior it provides: HTML forms collect user input and submit it to a client-side or server-side handler. Controls such as `input`, `select`, `textarea`, and `button` carry names, values, and validation rules. It can be useful when that behavior reduces a specific engineering or business problem, as in this example: A checkout form can collect an address and payment-related fields, then submit the data to a backend endpoint.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can distinguish a genuine benefit from a feature list and understand what the benefit costs.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Identify the behavior being provided: HTML forms collect user input and submit it to a client-side or server-side handler. Controls such as `input`, `select`, `textarea`, and `button` carry names, values, and validation rules.\n2. Connect it to the benefit shown by the example: A checkout form can collect an address and payment-related fields, then submit the data to a backend endpoint.\n3. State the trade-off: Client-side validation improves feedback but cannot replace server-side validation and authorization.\n4. Avoid claiming a benefit that is not supported by the actual workload.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would name the practical benefits first, tie each one to the behavior, and then give the main trade-off. That shows I understand why the feature is valuable rather than simply listing advantages.\n\n**Example :**\nA checkout form can collect an address and payment-related fields, then submit the data to a backend endpoint.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA web application may use HTML forms when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a checkout form can collect an address and payment-related fields, then submit the data to a backend endpoint. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Relying on intuition instead of the actual query plan.\n- Ignoring row counts and data distribution.\n- Fixing one query while creating a write, memory, or locking problem elsewhere.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain why the benefit matters for the workload. A benefit without a condition is usually too broad for a strong engineering answer.",
