    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen HTML forms misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to HTML forms. HTML forms collect user input and submit it to a client-side or server-side handler. Controls such as `input`, `select`, `textarea`, and `button` carry names, values, and validation rules.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls HTML forms: HTML forms collect user input and submit it to a client-side or server-side handler. Controls such as `input`, `select`, `textarea`, and `button` carry names, values, and validation rules.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: A checkout form can collect an address and payment-related fields, then submit the data to a backend endpoint.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: Client-side validation improves feedback but cannot replace server-side validation and authorization.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nA checkout form can collect an address and payment-related fields, then submit the data to a backend endpoint.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA web application may use HTML forms when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a checkout form can collect an address and payment-related fields, then submit the data to a backend endpoint. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Relying on intuition instead of the actual query plan.\n- Ignoring row counts and data distribution.\n- Fixing one query while creating a write, memory, or locking problem elsewhere.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
