    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nHTML forms is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. Client-side validation improves feedback but cannot replace server-side validation and authorization. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what HTML forms gives you: HTML forms collect user input and submit it to a client-side or server-side handler. Controls such as `input`, `select`, `textarea`, and `button` carry names, values, and validation rules.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: Client-side validation improves feedback but cannot replace server-side validation and authorization.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: A checkout form can collect an address and payment-related fields, then submit the data to a backend endpoint.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nA checkout form can collect an address and payment-related fields, then submit the data to a backend endpoint.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA web application may use HTML forms when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a checkout form can collect an address and payment-related fields, then submit the data to a backend endpoint. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Relying on intuition instead of the actual query plan.\n- Ignoring row counts and data distribution.\n- Fixing one query while creating a write, memory, or locking problem elsewhere.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nHTML forms is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. Client-side validation improves feedback but cannot replace server-side validation and authorization. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what HTML forms gives you: HTML forms collect user input and submit it to a client-side or server-side handler. Controls such as `input`, `select`, `textarea`, and `button` carry names, values, and validation rules.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: Client-side validation improves feedback but cannot replace server-side validation and authorization.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: A checkout form can collect an address and payment-related fields, then submit the data to a backend endpoint.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nA checkout form can collect an address and payment-related fields, then submit the data to a backend endpoint.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA web application may use HTML forms when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a checkout form can collect an address and payment-related fields, then submit the data to a backend endpoint. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Relying on intuition instead of the actual query plan.\n- Ignoring row counts and data distribution.\n- Fixing one query while creating a write, memory, or locking problem elsewhere.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
