    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nI would explain HTML forms in plain language first, then give the technical detail that distinguishes it from nearby concepts. The practical anchor is: A checkout form can collect an address and payment-related fields, then submit the data to a backend endpoint.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can communicate the concept clearly at an interview pace and adjust the depth when the interviewer asks a follow-up.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Give a one-sentence definition: HTML forms collect user input and submit it to a client-side or server-side handler. Controls such as `input`, `select`, `textarea`, and `button` carry names, values, and validation rules.\n2. Explain one important behavior or distinction.\n3. Use the example to make it concrete: A checkout form can collect an address and payment-related fields, then submit the data to a backend endpoint.\n4. Finish with the limitation: Client-side validation improves feedback but cannot replace server-side validation and authorization.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would keep the first answer concise, then wait for the interviewer to signal whether they want internals. If they go deeper, I would expand the mechanism and trade-offs rather than repeating the definition.\n\n**Example :**\nA checkout form can collect an address and payment-related fields, then submit the data to a backend endpoint.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA web application may use HTML forms when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a checkout form can collect an address and payment-related fields, then submit the data to a backend endpoint. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Relying on intuition instead of the actual query plan.\n- Ignoring row counts and data distribution.\n- Fixing one query while creating a write, memory, or locking problem elsewhere.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nPractice saying {c} aloud in your own words. A technically correct answer is weaker if you cannot explain it without reading a script.",
