    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “How would you diagnose HTML forms when performance degrades at production data volume?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to HTML forms.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: HTML forms collect user input and submit it to a client-side or server-side handler. Controls such as `input`, `select`, `textarea`, and `button` carry names, values, and validation rules.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: A checkout form can collect an address and payment-related fields, then submit the data to a backend endpoint.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: Client-side validation improves feedback but cannot replace server-side validation and authorization.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nA checkout form can collect an address and payment-related fields, then submit the data to a backend endpoint.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA web application may use HTML forms when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a checkout form can collect an address and payment-related fields, then submit the data to a backend endpoint. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Relying on intuition instead of the actual query plan.\n- Ignoring row counts and data distribution.\n- Fixing one query while creating a write, memory, or locking problem elsewhere.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
