    shortDescription: "Frequently asked HTML-CSS interview question about Accessibility.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWeb accessibility means making content and interactions usable by people with different abilities and assistive technologies. Semantic elements, labels, keyboard access, focus management, and sufficient contrast are common foundations.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Accessibility accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Web accessibility means making content and interactions usable by people with different abilities and assistive technologies. Semantic elements, labels, keyboard access, focus management, and sufficient contrast are common foundations.\n2. Use a concrete case: A form control needs an accessible name, keyboard operation, visible focus, and an error message that assistive technology can associate with the field.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: ARIA can help when native semantics are insufficient, but unnecessary ARIA can make an interface harder to use.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Accessibility first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA form control needs an accessible name, keyboard operation, visible focus, and an error message that assistive technology can associate with the field.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA web application may use Accessibility when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a form control needs an accessible name, keyboard operation, visible focus, and an error message that assistive technology can associate with the field. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Accessibility as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Accessibility.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Accessibility, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWeb accessibility means making content and interactions usable by people with different abilities and assistive technologies. Semantic elements, labels, keyboard access, focus management, and sufficient contrast are common foundations.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Accessibility accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Web accessibility means making content and interactions usable by people with different abilities and assistive technologies. Semantic elements, labels, keyboard access, focus management, and sufficient contrast are common foundations.\n2. Use a concrete case: A form control needs an accessible name, keyboard operation, visible focus, and an error message that assistive technology can associate with the field.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: ARIA can help when native semantics are insufficient, but unnecessary ARIA can make an interface harder to use.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Accessibility first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA form control needs an accessible name, keyboard operation, visible focus, and an error message that assistive technology can associate with the field.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA web application may use Accessibility when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a form control needs an accessible name, keyboard operation, visible focus, and an error message that assistive technology can associate with the field. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Accessibility as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Accessibility.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Accessibility, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
