    shortDescription: "Frequently asked HTML-CSS interview question about Accessibility.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nIt matters because web accessibility means making content and interactions usable by people with different abilities and assistive technologies. In practice, the example shows how that behavior is used: a form control needs an accessible name, keyboard operation, visible focus, and an error message that assistive technology can associate with the field.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe question is looking for reasoning rather than another definition. Explain the problem the concept solves, then say what would make you choose a different approach.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State the problem or need.\n2. Explain how Accessibility addresses it: Web accessibility means making content and interactions usable by people with different abilities and assistive technologies. Semantic elements, labels, keyboard access, focus management, and sufficient contrast are common foundations.\n3. Tie that benefit to the concrete situation: A form control needs an accessible name, keyboard operation, visible focus, and an error message that assistive technology can associate with the field.\n4. State the condition that can make the approach a poor fit: ARIA can help when native semantics are insufficient, but unnecessary ARIA can make an interface harder to use.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nAnswer the “why” directly, then connect it to a real engineering constraint. I would give one concrete situation and close by saying what would make me choose another approach.\n\n**Example :**\nA form control needs an accessible name, keyboard operation, visible focus, and an error message that assistive technology can associate with the field.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA web application may use Accessibility when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a form control needs an accessible name, keyboard operation, visible focus, and an error message that assistive technology can associate with the field. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Accessibility as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Accessibility.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nDo not repeat the definition as your whole answer. Explain the problem, the practical value, and the condition that would change your decision.",
