    shortDescription: "Frequently asked HTML-CSS interview question about Accessibility.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe main value of Accessibility comes from the behavior it provides: Web accessibility means making content and interactions usable by people with different abilities and assistive technologies. Semantic elements, labels, keyboard access, focus management, and sufficient contrast are common foundations. It can be useful when that behavior reduces a specific engineering or business problem, as in this example: A form control needs an accessible name, keyboard operation, visible focus, and an error message that assistive technology can associate with the field.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can distinguish a genuine benefit from a feature list and understand what the benefit costs.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Identify the behavior being provided: Web accessibility means making content and interactions usable by people with different abilities and assistive technologies. Semantic elements, labels, keyboard access, focus management, and sufficient contrast are common foundations.\n2. Connect it to the benefit shown by the example: A form control needs an accessible name, keyboard operation, visible focus, and an error message that assistive technology can associate with the field.\n3. State the trade-off: ARIA can help when native semantics are insufficient, but unnecessary ARIA can make an interface harder to use.\n4. Avoid claiming a benefit that is not supported by the actual workload.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would name the practical benefits first, tie each one to the behavior, and then give the main trade-off. That shows I understand why the feature is valuable rather than simply listing advantages.\n\n**Example :**\nA form control needs an accessible name, keyboard operation, visible focus, and an error message that assistive technology can associate with the field.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA web application may use Accessibility when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a form control needs an accessible name, keyboard operation, visible focus, and an error message that assistive technology can associate with the field. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Accessibility as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Accessibility.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain why the benefit matters for the workload. A benefit without a condition is usually too broad for a strong engineering answer.",
