    shortDescription: "Frequently asked HTML-CSS interview question about Selectors.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nCSS selectors identify the elements to which a rule applies. They include type, class, ID, attribute, pseudo-class, and relationship selectors.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Selectors accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: CSS selectors identify the elements to which a rule applies. They include type, class, ID, attribute, pseudo-class, and relationship selectors.\n2. Use a concrete case: `.error input` targets inputs inside an element with the `error` class, while `button:hover` targets a button in a hover state.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Broad selectors are convenient but can create unintended matches; overly specific selectors are difficult to override.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Selectors first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\n`.error input` targets inputs inside an element with the `error` class, while `button:hover` targets a button in a hover state.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA web application may use Selectors when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, `.error input` targets inputs inside an element with the `error` class, while `button:hover` targets a button in a hover state. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Selectors as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Selectors.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Selectors, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
