    question: "What is Specificity?",
    slug: "html-css-what-is-specificity-71",
    shortDescription: "Frequently asked HTML-CSS interview question about Specificity.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nCSS specificity is the priority used to resolve competing declarations when rules target the same property. IDs, classes/attributes/pseudo-classes, and element selectors contribute different weights, with source order resolving ties.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Specificity accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: CSS specificity is the priority used to resolve competing declarations when rules target the same property. IDs, classes/attributes/pseudo-classes, and element selectors contribute different weights, with source order resolving ties.\n2. Use a concrete case: If a class rule loses to an ID rule, increasing specificity may change the result, but reducing unnecessary specificity is usually easier to maintain.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: High specificity can make styles difficult to override; `!important` should not become the normal solution.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Specificity first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nIf a class rule loses to an ID rule, increasing specificity may change the result, but reducing unnecessary specificity is usually easier to maintain.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA web application may use Specificity when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, if a class rule loses to an ID rule, increasing specificity may change the result, but reducing unnecessary specificity is usually easier to maintain. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Specificity as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Specificity.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Specificity, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
