    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe main value of Specificity comes from the behavior it provides: CSS specificity is the priority used to resolve competing declarations when rules target the same property. IDs, classes/attributes/pseudo-classes, and element selectors contribute different weights, with source order resolving ties. It can be useful when that behavior reduces a specific engineering or business problem, as in this example: If a class rule loses to an ID rule, increasing specificity may change the result, but reducing unnecessary specificity is usually easier to maintain.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can distinguish a genuine benefit from a feature list and understand what the benefit costs.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Identify the behavior being provided: CSS specificity is the priority used to resolve competing declarations when rules target the same property. IDs, classes/attributes/pseudo-classes, and element selectors contribute different weights, with source order resolving ties.\n2. Connect it to the benefit shown by the example: If a class rule loses to an ID rule, increasing specificity may change the result, but reducing unnecessary specificity is usually easier to maintain.\n3. State the trade-off: High specificity can make styles difficult to override; `!important` should not become the normal solution.\n4. Avoid claiming a benefit that is not supported by the actual workload.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would name the practical benefits first, tie each one to the behavior, and then give the main trade-off. That shows I understand why the feature is valuable rather than simply listing advantages.\n\n**Example :**\nIf a class rule loses to an ID rule, increasing specificity may change the result, but reducing unnecessary specificity is usually easier to maintain.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA web application may use Specificity when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, if a class rule loses to an ID rule, increasing specificity may change the result, but reducing unnecessary specificity is usually easier to maintain. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Specificity as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Specificity.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain why the benefit matters for the workload. A benefit without a condition is usually too broad for a strong engineering answer.",
