    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nI would use Specificity when the problem matches the behavior it provides: CSS specificity is the priority used to resolve competing declarations when rules target the same property. IDs, classes/attributes/pseudo-classes, and element selectors contribute different weights, with source order resolving ties. A concrete application is If a class rule loses to an ID rule, increasing specificity may change the result, but reducing unnecessary specificity is usually easier to maintain.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you can translate the concept into an engineering decision rather than reciting its definition.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Identify the requirement.\n2. Check that Specificity provides the required behavior: CSS specificity is the priority used to resolve competing declarations when rules target the same property. IDs, classes/attributes/pseudo-classes, and element selectors contribute different weights, with source order resolving ties.\n3. Apply it in a concrete case: If a class rule loses to an ID rule, increasing specificity may change the result, but reducing unnecessary specificity is usually easier to maintain.\n4. Reassess the choice if the workload or constraints change: High specificity can make styles difficult to override; `!important` should not become the normal solution.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the requirement, then explain why Specificity fits it. I would give the concrete example and mention the main limitation so the answer shows judgment, not just tool familiarity.\n\n**Example :**\nIf a class rule loses to an ID rule, increasing specificity may change the result, but reducing unnecessary specificity is usually easier to maintain.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA web application may use Specificity when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, if a class rule loses to an ID rule, increasing specificity may change the result, but reducing unnecessary specificity is usually easier to maintain. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Specificity as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Specificity.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nDescribe the requirement that led you to choose it. “I would use it because it is popular” is much weaker than explaining the actual problem.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nI would use Specificity when the problem matches the behavior it provides: CSS specificity is the priority used to resolve competing declarations when rules target the same property. IDs, classes/attributes/pseudo-classes, and element selectors contribute different weights, with source order resolving ties. A concrete application is If a class rule loses to an ID rule, increasing specificity may change the result, but reducing unnecessary specificity is usually easier to maintain.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you can translate the concept into an engineering decision rather than reciting its definition.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Identify the requirement.\n2. Check that Specificity provides the required behavior: CSS specificity is the priority used to resolve competing declarations when rules target the same property. IDs, classes/attributes/pseudo-classes, and element selectors contribute different weights, with source order resolving ties.\n3. Apply it in a concrete case: If a class rule loses to an ID rule, increasing specificity may change the result, but reducing unnecessary specificity is usually easier to maintain.\n4. Reassess the choice if the workload or constraints change: High specificity can make styles difficult to override; `!important` should not become the normal solution.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the requirement, then explain why Specificity fits it. I would give the concrete example and mention the main limitation so the answer shows judgment, not just tool familiarity.\n\n**Example :**\nIf a class rule loses to an ID rule, increasing specificity may change the result, but reducing unnecessary specificity is usually easier to maintain.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA web application may use Specificity when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, if a class rule loses to an ID rule, increasing specificity may change the result, but reducing unnecessary specificity is usually easier to maintain. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Specificity as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Specificity.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nDescribe the requirement that led you to choose it. “I would use it because it is popular” is much weaker than explaining the actual problem.",
