    shortDescription: "Frequently asked HTML-CSS interview question about Specificity.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nSpecificity is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. High specificity can make styles difficult to override; `!important` should not become the normal solution. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what Specificity gives you: CSS specificity is the priority used to resolve competing declarations when rules target the same property. IDs, classes/attributes/pseudo-classes, and element selectors contribute different weights, with source order resolving ties.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: High specificity can make styles difficult to override; `!important` should not become the normal solution.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: If a class rule loses to an ID rule, increasing specificity may change the result, but reducing unnecessary specificity is usually easier to maintain.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nIf a class rule loses to an ID rule, increasing specificity may change the result, but reducing unnecessary specificity is usually easier to maintain.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA web application may use Specificity when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, if a class rule loses to an ID rule, increasing specificity may change the result, but reducing unnecessary specificity is usually easier to maintain. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Specificity as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Specificity.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
