    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “What would you change about Specificity when the system grows or an important assumption stops being true?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to Specificity.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: CSS specificity is the priority used to resolve competing declarations when rules target the same property. IDs, classes/attributes/pseudo-classes, and element selectors contribute different weights, with source order resolving ties.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: If a class rule loses to an ID rule, increasing specificity may change the result, but reducing unnecessary specificity is usually easier to maintain.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: High specificity can make styles difficult to override; `!important` should not become the normal solution.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nIf a class rule loses to an ID rule, increasing specificity may change the result, but reducing unnecessary specificity is usually easier to maintain.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA web application may use Specificity when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, if a class rule loses to an ID rule, increasing specificity may change the result, but reducing unnecessary specificity is usually easier to maintain. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Specificity as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Specificity.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
