    question: "What is Box model?",
    slug: "html-css-what-is-box-model-81",
    shortDescription: "Frequently asked HTML-CSS interview question about Box model.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe CSS box model describes an element as content surrounded by padding, border, and margin. The `box-sizing` property changes how declared width and height are interpreted.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Box model accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: The CSS box model describes an element as content surrounded by padding, border, and margin. The `box-sizing` property changes how declared width and height are interpreted.\n2. Use a concrete case: With `box-sizing: border-box`, a 300px-wide input includes its padding and border inside the 300px size.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Margins and padding affect spacing differently, and intrinsic content can still affect the final layout.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Box model first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nWith `box-sizing: border-box`, a 300px-wide input includes its padding and border inside the 300px size.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA web application may use Box model when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, with `box-sizing: border-box`, a 300px-wide input includes its padding and border inside the 300px size. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Box model as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Box model.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Box model, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
