    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nI would use Box model when the problem matches the behavior it provides: The CSS box model describes an element as content surrounded by padding, border, and margin. The `box-sizing` property changes how declared width and height are interpreted. A concrete application is With `box-sizing: border-box`, a 300px-wide input includes its padding and border inside the 300px size.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you can translate the concept into an engineering decision rather than reciting its definition.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Identify the requirement.\n2. Check that Box model provides the required behavior: The CSS box model describes an element as content surrounded by padding, border, and margin. The `box-sizing` property changes how declared width and height are interpreted.\n3. Apply it in a concrete case: With `box-sizing: border-box`, a 300px-wide input includes its padding and border inside the 300px size.\n4. Reassess the choice if the workload or constraints change: Margins and padding affect spacing differently, and intrinsic content can still affect the final layout.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the requirement, then explain why Box model fits it. I would give the concrete example and mention the main limitation so the answer shows judgment, not just tool familiarity.\n\n**Example :**\nWith `box-sizing: border-box`, a 300px-wide input includes its padding and border inside the 300px size.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA web application may use Box model when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, with `box-sizing: border-box`, a 300px-wide input includes its padding and border inside the 300px size. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Box model as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Box model.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nDescribe the requirement that led you to choose it. “I would use it because it is popular” is much weaker than explaining the actual problem.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nI would use Box model when the problem matches the behavior it provides: The CSS box model describes an element as content surrounded by padding, border, and margin. The `box-sizing` property changes how declared width and height are interpreted. A concrete application is With `box-sizing: border-box`, a 300px-wide input includes its padding and border inside the 300px size.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you can translate the concept into an engineering decision rather than reciting its definition.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Identify the requirement.\n2. Check that Box model provides the required behavior: The CSS box model describes an element as content surrounded by padding, border, and margin. The `box-sizing` property changes how declared width and height are interpreted.\n3. Apply it in a concrete case: With `box-sizing: border-box`, a 300px-wide input includes its padding and border inside the 300px size.\n4. Reassess the choice if the workload or constraints change: Margins and padding affect spacing differently, and intrinsic content can still affect the final layout.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the requirement, then explain why Box model fits it. I would give the concrete example and mention the main limitation so the answer shows judgment, not just tool familiarity.\n\n**Example :**\nWith `box-sizing: border-box`, a 300px-wide input includes its padding and border inside the 300px size.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA web application may use Box model when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, with `box-sizing: border-box`, a 300px-wide input includes its padding and border inside the 300px size. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Box model as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Box model.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nDescribe the requirement that led you to choose it. “I would use it because it is popular” is much weaker than explaining the actual problem.",
