    shortDescription: "Frequently asked HTML-CSS interview question about Box model.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nBox model is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. Margins and padding affect spacing differently, and intrinsic content can still affect the final layout. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what Box model gives you: The CSS box model describes an element as content surrounded by padding, border, and margin. The `box-sizing` property changes how declared width and height are interpreted.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: Margins and padding affect spacing differently, and intrinsic content can still affect the final layout.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: With `box-sizing: border-box`, a 300px-wide input includes its padding and border inside the 300px size.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nWith `box-sizing: border-box`, a 300px-wide input includes its padding and border inside the 300px size.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA web application may use Box model when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, with `box-sizing: border-box`, a 300px-wide input includes its padding and border inside the 300px size. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Box model as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Box model.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
