    slug: "html-css-what-is-flexbox-101",
    shortDescription: "Frequently asked HTML-CSS interview question about Flexbox.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nFlexbox is a one-dimensional layout model for arranging items along a row or column. It provides alignment, distribution, wrapping, and flexible sizing.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Flexbox accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Flexbox is a one-dimensional layout model for arranging items along a row or column. It provides alignment, distribution, wrapping, and flexible sizing.\n2. Use a concrete case: A toolbar can use `display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center` to align controls without absolute positioning.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Flexbox is excellent for one dimension; complex two-dimensional layouts are often clearer with Grid.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Flexbox first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA toolbar can use `display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center` to align controls without absolute positioning.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA web application may use Flexbox when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a toolbar can use `display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center` to align controls without absolute positioning. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Flexbox as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Flexbox.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Flexbox, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
