    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nIt matters because flexbox is a one-dimensional layout model for arranging items along a row or column. In practice, the example shows how that behavior is used: a toolbar can use `display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center` to align controls without absolute positioning.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe question is looking for reasoning rather than another definition. Explain the problem the concept solves, then say what would make you choose a different approach.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State the problem or need.\n2. Explain how Flexbox addresses it: Flexbox is a one-dimensional layout model for arranging items along a row or column. It provides alignment, distribution, wrapping, and flexible sizing.\n3. Tie that benefit to the concrete situation: A toolbar can use `display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center` to align controls without absolute positioning.\n4. State the condition that can make the approach a poor fit: Flexbox is excellent for one dimension; complex two-dimensional layouts are often clearer with Grid.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nAnswer the “why” directly, then connect it to a real engineering constraint. I would give one concrete situation and close by saying what would make me choose another approach.\n\n**Example :**\nA toolbar can use `display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center` to align controls without absolute positioning.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA web application may use Flexbox when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a toolbar can use `display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center` to align controls without absolute positioning. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Flexbox as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Flexbox.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nDo not repeat the definition as your whole answer. Explain the problem, the practical value, and the condition that would change your decision.",
