    shortDescription: "Frequently asked HTML-CSS interview question about Flexbox.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nI would explain Flexbox in plain language first, then give the technical detail that distinguishes it from nearby concepts. The practical anchor is: A toolbar can use `display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center` to align controls without absolute positioning.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can communicate the concept clearly at an interview pace and adjust the depth when the interviewer asks a follow-up.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Give a one-sentence definition: Flexbox is a one-dimensional layout model for arranging items along a row or column. It provides alignment, distribution, wrapping, and flexible sizing.\n2. Explain one important behavior or distinction.\n3. Use the example to make it concrete: A toolbar can use `display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center` to align controls without absolute positioning.\n4. Finish with the limitation: Flexbox is excellent for one dimension; complex two-dimensional layouts are often clearer with Grid.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would keep the first answer concise, then wait for the interviewer to signal whether they want internals. If they go deeper, I would expand the mechanism and trade-offs rather than repeating the definition.\n\n**Example :**\nA toolbar can use `display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center` to align controls without absolute positioning.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA web application may use Flexbox when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a toolbar can use `display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center` to align controls without absolute positioning. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Flexbox as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Flexbox.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nPractice saying {c} aloud in your own words. A technically correct answer is weaker if you cannot explain it without reading a script.",
