    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “What would you change about Flexbox when the system grows or an important assumption stops being true?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to Flexbox.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: Flexbox is a one-dimensional layout model for arranging items along a row or column. It provides alignment, distribution, wrapping, and flexible sizing.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: A toolbar can use `display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center` to align controls without absolute positioning.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: Flexbox is excellent for one dimension; complex two-dimensional layouts are often clearer with Grid.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nA toolbar can use `display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center` to align controls without absolute positioning.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA web application may use Flexbox when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a toolbar can use `display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center` to align controls without absolute positioning. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Flexbox as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Flexbox.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “What would you change about Flexbox when the system grows or an important assumption stops being true?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to Flexbox.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: Flexbox is a one-dimensional layout model for arranging items along a row or column. It provides alignment, distribution, wrapping, and flexible sizing.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: A toolbar can use `display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center` to align controls without absolute positioning.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: Flexbox is excellent for one dimension; complex two-dimensional layouts are often clearer with Grid.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nA toolbar can use `display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center` to align controls without absolute positioning.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA web application may use Flexbox when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a toolbar can use `display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center` to align controls without absolute positioning. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Flexbox as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Flexbox.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
