    shortDescription: "Frequently asked HTML-CSS interview question about Grid.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nCSS Grid is a two-dimensional layout system that controls rows and columns. It is useful when both axes matter to the layout.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Grid accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: CSS Grid is a two-dimensional layout system that controls rows and columns. It is useful when both axes matter to the layout.\n2. Use a concrete case: A dashboard can define a grid of columns and rows and place cards into explicit or auto-generated areas.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Grid provides strong layout control but can be more configuration than a simple flex row needs.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Grid first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA dashboard can define a grid of columns and rows and place cards into explicit or auto-generated areas.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA web application may use Grid when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a dashboard can define a grid of columns and rows and place cards into explicit or auto-generated areas. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Grid as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Grid.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Grid, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
