    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nGrid is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. Grid provides strong layout control but can be more configuration than a simple flex row needs. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what Grid gives you: CSS Grid is a two-dimensional layout system that controls rows and columns. It is useful when both axes matter to the layout.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: Grid provides strong layout control but can be more configuration than a simple flex row needs.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: A dashboard can define a grid of columns and rows and place cards into explicit or auto-generated areas.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nA dashboard can define a grid of columns and rows and place cards into explicit or auto-generated areas.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA web application may use Grid when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a dashboard can define a grid of columns and rows and place cards into explicit or auto-generated areas. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Grid as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Grid.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
