    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nCSS positioning controls how an element is placed relative to normal flow or an ancestor, using values such as `relative`, `absolute`, `fixed`, and `sticky`.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Positioning accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: CSS positioning controls how an element is placed relative to normal flow or an ancestor, using values such as `relative`, `absolute`, `fixed`, and `sticky`.\n2. Use a concrete case: A tooltip can be positioned absolutely inside a relatively positioned container.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Positioning can solve local layout problems, but overusing absolute or fixed positioning can make responsive layouts brittle.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Positioning first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA tooltip can be positioned absolutely inside a relatively positioned container.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA web application may use Positioning when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a tooltip can be positioned absolutely inside a relatively positioned container. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Positioning as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Positioning.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Positioning, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
