    shortDescription: "Frequently asked HTML-CSS interview question about Positioning.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe main value of Positioning comes from the behavior it provides: CSS positioning controls how an element is placed relative to normal flow or an ancestor, using values such as `relative`, `absolute`, `fixed`, and `sticky`. It can be useful when that behavior reduces a specific engineering or business problem, as in this example: A tooltip can be positioned absolutely inside a relatively positioned container.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can distinguish a genuine benefit from a feature list and understand what the benefit costs.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Identify the behavior being provided: CSS positioning controls how an element is placed relative to normal flow or an ancestor, using values such as `relative`, `absolute`, `fixed`, and `sticky`.\n2. Connect it to the benefit shown by the example: A tooltip can be positioned absolutely inside a relatively positioned container.\n3. State the trade-off: Positioning can solve local layout problems, but overusing absolute or fixed positioning can make responsive layouts brittle.\n4. Avoid claiming a benefit that is not supported by the actual workload.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would name the practical benefits first, tie each one to the behavior, and then give the main trade-off. That shows I understand why the feature is valuable rather than simply listing advantages.\n\n**Example :**\nA tooltip can be positioned absolutely inside a relatively positioned container.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA web application may use Positioning when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a tooltip can be positioned absolutely inside a relatively positioned container. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Positioning as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Positioning.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain why the benefit matters for the workload. A benefit without a condition is usually too broad for a strong engineering answer.",
