    shortDescription: "Frequently asked HTML-CSS interview question about Positioning.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Positioning misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Positioning. CSS positioning controls how an element is placed relative to normal flow or an ancestor, using values such as `relative`, `absolute`, `fixed`, and `sticky`.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Positioning: CSS positioning controls how an element is placed relative to normal flow or an ancestor, using values such as `relative`, `absolute`, `fixed`, and `sticky`.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: A tooltip can be positioned absolutely inside a relatively positioned container.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: Positioning can solve local layout problems, but overusing absolute or fixed positioning can make responsive layouts brittle.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nA tooltip can be positioned absolutely inside a relatively positioned container.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA web application may use Positioning when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a tooltip can be positioned absolutely inside a relatively positioned container. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Positioning as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Positioning.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
