    slug: "html-css-what-is-responsive-design-131",
    shortDescription: "Frequently asked HTML-CSS interview question about Responsive design.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nResponsive design adapts layout and content to different viewport sizes, input methods, and device characteristics. Flexible sizing and responsive layout techniques reduce device-specific hacks.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Responsive design accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Responsive design adapts layout and content to different viewport sizes, input methods, and device characteristics. Flexible sizing and responsive layout techniques reduce device-specific hacks.\n2. Use a concrete case: A product grid can change from four columns on a wide screen to two or one column on narrower screens.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Responsive behavior should follow content constraints, not only a list of device widths.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Responsive design first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA product grid can change from four columns on a wide screen to two or one column on narrower screens.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA web application may use Responsive design when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a product grid can change from four columns on a wide screen to two or one column on narrower screens. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Responsive design as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Responsive design.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Responsive design, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
