    slug: "html-css-what-are-the-common-mistakes-with-responsive-design-135",
    shortDescription: "Frequently asked HTML-CSS interview question about Responsive design.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe most common mistakes with Responsive design are usually misunderstandings of its actual behavior, poor handling of its constraints, or using it where a simpler approach would be enough. The important cases are below.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you have enough practical experience to recognize failure patterns rather than only describe the happy path.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Know the intended behavior: Responsive design adapts layout and content to different viewport sizes, input methods, and device characteristics. Flexible sizing and responsive layout techniques reduce device-specific hacks.\n2. Watch for these common failure patterns:\n- Treating Responsive design as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Responsive design.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n3. Use the example to check whether the implementation really behaves as expected: A product grid can change from four columns on a wide screen to two or one column on narrower screens.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would name two or three mistakes that are realistic for this concept and briefly explain why each one matters. I would finish with the one mistake I see most often in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nA product grid can change from four columns on a wide screen to two or one column on narrower screens.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA web application may use Responsive design when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a product grid can change from four columns on a wide screen to two or one column on narrower screens. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Responsive design as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Responsive design.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nChoose mistakes that are specific to Responsive design. Avoid generic answers such as “not testing enough” unless you explain exactly what can go wrong.",
