    shortDescription: "Frequently asked HTML-CSS interview question about Responsive design.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nI would use Responsive design when the problem matches the behavior it provides: Responsive design adapts layout and content to different viewport sizes, input methods, and device characteristics. Flexible sizing and responsive layout techniques reduce device-specific hacks. A concrete application is A product grid can change from four columns on a wide screen to two or one column on narrower screens.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you can translate the concept into an engineering decision rather than reciting its definition.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Identify the requirement.\n2. Check that Responsive design provides the required behavior: Responsive design adapts layout and content to different viewport sizes, input methods, and device characteristics. Flexible sizing and responsive layout techniques reduce device-specific hacks.\n3. Apply it in a concrete case: A product grid can change from four columns on a wide screen to two or one column on narrower screens.\n4. Reassess the choice if the workload or constraints change: Responsive behavior should follow content constraints, not only a list of device widths.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the requirement, then explain why Responsive design fits it. I would give the concrete example and mention the main limitation so the answer shows judgment, not just tool familiarity.\n\n**Example :**\nA product grid can change from four columns on a wide screen to two or one column on narrower screens.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA web application may use Responsive design when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a product grid can change from four columns on a wide screen to two or one column on narrower screens. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Responsive design as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Responsive design.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nDescribe the requirement that led you to choose it. “I would use it because it is popular” is much weaker than explaining the actual problem.",
