    shortDescription: "Frequently asked HTML-CSS interview question about Media queries.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nI would use Media queries when the problem matches the behavior it provides: Media queries apply CSS based on conditions such as viewport width, resolution, orientation, or user preferences. A concrete application is A navigation layout can switch to a stacked arrangement when the available width becomes too small for the desktop layout.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you can translate the concept into an engineering decision rather than reciting its definition.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Identify the requirement.\n2. Check that Media queries provides the required behavior: Media queries apply CSS based on conditions such as viewport width, resolution, orientation, or user preferences.\n3. Apply it in a concrete case: A navigation layout can switch to a stacked arrangement when the available width becomes too small for the desktop layout.\n4. Reassess the choice if the workload or constraints change: Too many breakpoint-specific rules can create maintenance problems; fluid CSS often reduces them.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the requirement, then explain why Media queries fits it. I would give the concrete example and mention the main limitation so the answer shows judgment, not just tool familiarity.\n\n**Example :**\nA navigation layout can switch to a stacked arrangement when the available width becomes too small for the desktop layout.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA web application may use Media queries when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a navigation layout can switch to a stacked arrangement when the available width becomes too small for the desktop layout. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Media queries as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Media queries.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nDescribe the requirement that led you to choose it. “I would use it because it is popular” is much weaker than explaining the actual problem.",
