    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Media queries misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Media queries. Media queries apply CSS based on conditions such as viewport width, resolution, orientation, or user preferences.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Media queries: Media queries apply CSS based on conditions such as viewport width, resolution, orientation, or user preferences.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: A navigation layout can switch to a stacked arrangement when the available width becomes too small for the desktop layout.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: Too many breakpoint-specific rules can create maintenance problems; fluid CSS often reduces them.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nA navigation layout can switch to a stacked arrangement when the available width becomes too small for the desktop layout.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA web application may use Media queries when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a navigation layout can switch to a stacked arrangement when the available width becomes too small for the desktop layout. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Media queries as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Media queries.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
