    shortDescription: "Frequently asked HTML-CSS interview question about Media queries.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nMedia queries is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. Too many breakpoint-specific rules can create maintenance problems; fluid CSS often reduces them. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what Media queries gives you: Media queries apply CSS based on conditions such as viewport width, resolution, orientation, or user preferences.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: Too many breakpoint-specific rules can create maintenance problems; fluid CSS often reduces them.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: A navigation layout can switch to a stacked arrangement when the available width becomes too small for the desktop layout.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nA navigation layout can switch to a stacked arrangement when the available width becomes too small for the desktop layout.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA web application may use Media queries when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a navigation layout can switch to a stacked arrangement when the available width becomes too small for the desktop layout. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Media queries as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Media queries.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
