    shortDescription: "Frequently asked HTML-CSS interview question about Units.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nCSS units define dimensions, including absolute units such as `px` and relative units such as `%`, `rem`, `em`, `vw`, and `vh`.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Units accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: CSS units define dimensions, including absolute units such as `px` and relative units such as `%`, `rem`, `em`, `vw`, and `vh`.\n2. Use a concrete case: Use `rem` for scalable typography and percentages or grid fractions for fluid layout where appropriate.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: The right unit depends on whether the value should track font size, containing space, or the viewport.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Units first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nUse `rem` for scalable typography and percentages or grid fractions for fluid layout where appropriate.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA web application may use Units when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, use `rem` for scalable typography and percentages or grid fractions for fluid layout where appropriate. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Units as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Units.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Units, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
