    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Units misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Units. CSS units define dimensions, including absolute units such as `px` and relative units such as `%`, `rem`, `em`, `vw`, and `vh`.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Units: CSS units define dimensions, including absolute units such as `px` and relative units such as `%`, `rem`, `em`, `vw`, and `vh`.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: Use `rem` for scalable typography and percentages or grid fractions for fluid layout where appropriate.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: The right unit depends on whether the value should track font size, containing space, or the viewport.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nUse `rem` for scalable typography and percentages or grid fractions for fluid layout where appropriate.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA web application may use Units when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, use `rem` for scalable typography and percentages or grid fractions for fluid layout where appropriate. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Units as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Units.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
