    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “What would you change about Pseudo classes when the system grows or an important assumption stops being true?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to Pseudo classes.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: CSS pseudo-classes target element states or conditions, such as `:hover`, `:focus`, `:checked`, `:disabled`, and `:nth-child()`.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: Use `:focus-visible` to make keyboard focus visible without forcing the same outline in every pointer interaction.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: State selectors are useful, but interaction-critical behavior should remain understandable and accessible.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nUse `:focus-visible` to make keyboard focus visible without forcing the same outline in every pointer interaction.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA web application may use Pseudo classes when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, use `:focus-visible` to make keyboard focus visible without forcing the same outline in every pointer interaction. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Pseudo classes as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Pseudo classes.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
