    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nCSS animations and transitions change visual properties over time. They can be used for state changes, feedback, and visual continuity.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Animations accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: CSS animations and transitions change visual properties over time. They can be used for state changes, feedback, and visual continuity.\n2. Use a concrete case: A button can transition its background and transform slightly on hover instead of changing abruptly.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Animations can improve feedback but should respect reduced-motion preferences and avoid unnecessary work.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Animations first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA button can transition its background and transform slightly on hover instead of changing abruptly.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA web application may use Animations when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a button can transition its background and transform slightly on hover instead of changing abruptly. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Animations as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Animations.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Animations, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
