    shortDescription: "Frequently asked HTML-CSS interview question about Web performance.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWeb performance is the time and resources required to load, render, and respond to interactions. Important factors include network transfer, JavaScript work, rendering, caching, and image delivery.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Web performance accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Web performance is the time and resources required to load, render, and respond to interactions. Important factors include network transfer, JavaScript work, rendering, caching, and image delivery.\n2. Use a concrete case: A landing page can reduce blocking JavaScript, compress images, and cache static assets to improve initial loading.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Optimizing one metric can hurt another, so performance work should be measured against real user behavior.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Web performance first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA landing page can reduce blocking JavaScript, compress images, and cache static assets to improve initial loading.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA web application may use Web performance when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a landing page can reduce blocking JavaScript, compress images, and cache static assets to improve initial loading. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Relying on intuition instead of the actual query plan.\n- Ignoring row counts and data distribution.\n- Fixing one query while creating a write, memory, or locking problem elsewhere.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Web performance, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
