    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe main value of Web performance comes from the behavior it provides: Web performance is the time and resources required to load, render, and respond to interactions. Important factors include network transfer, JavaScript work, rendering, caching, and image delivery. It can be useful when that behavior reduces a specific engineering or business problem, as in this example: A landing page can reduce blocking JavaScript, compress images, and cache static assets to improve initial loading.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can distinguish a genuine benefit from a feature list and understand what the benefit costs.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Identify the behavior being provided: Web performance is the time and resources required to load, render, and respond to interactions. Important factors include network transfer, JavaScript work, rendering, caching, and image delivery.\n2. Connect it to the benefit shown by the example: A landing page can reduce blocking JavaScript, compress images, and cache static assets to improve initial loading.\n3. State the trade-off: Optimizing one metric can hurt another, so performance work should be measured against real user behavior.\n4. Avoid claiming a benefit that is not supported by the actual workload.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would name the practical benefits first, tie each one to the behavior, and then give the main trade-off. That shows I understand why the feature is valuable rather than simply listing advantages.\n\n**Example :**\nA landing page can reduce blocking JavaScript, compress images, and cache static assets to improve initial loading.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA web application may use Web performance when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a landing page can reduce blocking JavaScript, compress images, and cache static assets to improve initial loading. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Relying on intuition instead of the actual query plan.\n- Ignoring row counts and data distribution.\n- Fixing one query while creating a write, memory, or locking problem elsewhere.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain why the benefit matters for the workload. A benefit without a condition is usually too broad for a strong engineering answer.",
