    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nI would use Web performance when the problem matches the behavior it provides: Web performance is the time and resources required to load, render, and respond to interactions. Important factors include network transfer, JavaScript work, rendering, caching, and image delivery. A concrete application is A landing page can reduce blocking JavaScript, compress images, and cache static assets to improve initial loading.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you can translate the concept into an engineering decision rather than reciting its definition.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Identify the requirement.\n2. Check that Web performance provides the required behavior: Web performance is the time and resources required to load, render, and respond to interactions. Important factors include network transfer, JavaScript work, rendering, caching, and image delivery.\n3. Apply it in a concrete case: A landing page can reduce blocking JavaScript, compress images, and cache static assets to improve initial loading.\n4. Reassess the choice if the workload or constraints change: Optimizing one metric can hurt another, so performance work should be measured against real user behavior.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the requirement, then explain why Web performance fits it. I would give the concrete example and mention the main limitation so the answer shows judgment, not just tool familiarity.\n\n**Example :**\nA landing page can reduce blocking JavaScript, compress images, and cache static assets to improve initial loading.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA web application may use Web performance when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a landing page can reduce blocking JavaScript, compress images, and cache static assets to improve initial loading. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Relying on intuition instead of the actual query plan.\n- Ignoring row counts and data distribution.\n- Fixing one query while creating a write, memory, or locking problem elsewhere.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nDescribe the requirement that led you to choose it. “I would use it because it is popular” is much weaker than explaining the actual problem.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nI would use Web performance when the problem matches the behavior it provides: Web performance is the time and resources required to load, render, and respond to interactions. Important factors include network transfer, JavaScript work, rendering, caching, and image delivery. A concrete application is A landing page can reduce blocking JavaScript, compress images, and cache static assets to improve initial loading.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you can translate the concept into an engineering decision rather than reciting its definition.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Identify the requirement.\n2. Check that Web performance provides the required behavior: Web performance is the time and resources required to load, render, and respond to interactions. Important factors include network transfer, JavaScript work, rendering, caching, and image delivery.\n3. Apply it in a concrete case: A landing page can reduce blocking JavaScript, compress images, and cache static assets to improve initial loading.\n4. Reassess the choice if the workload or constraints change: Optimizing one metric can hurt another, so performance work should be measured against real user behavior.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the requirement, then explain why Web performance fits it. I would give the concrete example and mention the main limitation so the answer shows judgment, not just tool familiarity.\n\n**Example :**\nA landing page can reduce blocking JavaScript, compress images, and cache static assets to improve initial loading.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA web application may use Web performance when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a landing page can reduce blocking JavaScript, compress images, and cache static assets to improve initial loading. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Relying on intuition instead of the actual query plan.\n- Ignoring row counts and data distribution.\n- Fixing one query while creating a write, memory, or locking problem elsewhere.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nDescribe the requirement that led you to choose it. “I would use it because it is popular” is much weaker than explaining the actual problem.",
    explanation: "How would you use Web performance in a real project? is a core HTML-CSS interview concept. A strong answer should define the concept clearly, explain how it works, show where it is useful, and discuss practical trade-offs. The candidate should connect the explanation to a realistic engineering or business scenario and distinguish the concept from closely related alternatives.",
