    slug: "html-css-what-are-the-main-trade-offs-of-web-performance-198",
    shortDescription: "Frequently asked HTML-CSS interview question about Web performance.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWeb performance is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. Optimizing one metric can hurt another, so performance work should be measured against real user behavior. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what Web performance gives you: Web performance is the time and resources required to load, render, and respond to interactions. Important factors include network transfer, JavaScript work, rendering, caching, and image delivery.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: Optimizing one metric can hurt another, so performance work should be measured against real user behavior.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: A landing page can reduce blocking JavaScript, compress images, and cache static assets to improve initial loading.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nA landing page can reduce blocking JavaScript, compress images, and cache static assets to improve initial loading.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA web application may use Web performance when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a landing page can reduce blocking JavaScript, compress images, and cache static assets to improve initial loading. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Relying on intuition instead of the actual query plan.\n- Ignoring row counts and data distribution.\n- Fixing one query while creating a write, memory, or locking problem elsewhere.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
