    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “How would you diagnose Web performance when performance degrades at production data volume?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to Web performance.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: Web performance is the time and resources required to load, render, and respond to interactions. Important factors include network transfer, JavaScript work, rendering, caching, and image delivery.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: A landing page can reduce blocking JavaScript, compress images, and cache static assets to improve initial loading.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: Optimizing one metric can hurt another, so performance work should be measured against real user behavior.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nA landing page can reduce blocking JavaScript, compress images, and cache static assets to improve initial loading.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA web application may use Web performance when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a landing page can reduce blocking JavaScript, compress images, and cache static assets to improve initial loading. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Relying on intuition instead of the actual query plan.\n- Ignoring row counts and data distribution.\n- Fixing one query while creating a write, memory, or locking problem elsewhere.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “How would you diagnose Web performance when performance degrades at production data volume?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to Web performance.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: Web performance is the time and resources required to load, render, and respond to interactions. Important factors include network transfer, JavaScript work, rendering, caching, and image delivery.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: A landing page can reduce blocking JavaScript, compress images, and cache static assets to improve initial loading.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: Optimizing one metric can hurt another, so performance work should be measured against real user behavior.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nA landing page can reduce blocking JavaScript, compress images, and cache static assets to improve initial loading.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA web application may use Web performance when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a landing page can reduce blocking JavaScript, compress images, and cache static assets to improve initial loading. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Relying on intuition instead of the actual query plan.\n- Ignoring row counts and data distribution.\n- Fixing one query while creating a write, memory, or locking problem elsewhere.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
