    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe usual flow for Git workflows is to start with the inputs or conditions, apply the behavior described here, and verify the resulting state. A Git workflow defines how branches, reviews, merges, releases, and hotfixes are managed by a team.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the mechanism and can explain a sequence, not just the end result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Establish the starting condition.\n2. Apply the mechanism: A Git workflow defines how branches, reviews, merges, releases, and hotfixes are managed by a team.\n3. Verify the result using the concrete case: A trunk-based team may use short-lived branches and frequent merges while protecting the main branch with CI checks.\n4. Watch the trade-off or failure condition: The best workflow depends on release cadence, team size, risk, and deployment practices.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nWalk through the normal sequence in order. Explain what changes at each important step, then mention how you would verify the result and what you would inspect if the operation failed.\n\n**Example :**\nA trunk-based team may use short-lived branches and frequent merges while protecting the main branch with CI checks.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software delivery pipeline may use Git workflows when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a trunk-based team may use short-lived branches and frequent merges while protecting the main branch with CI checks. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Git workflows as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Git workflows.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the steps. That is more useful than listing commands or buzzwords.",
