    shortDescription: "Frequently asked DevOps interview question about Git workflows.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Git workflows misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Git workflows. A Git workflow defines how branches, reviews, merges, releases, and hotfixes are managed by a team.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Git workflows: A Git workflow defines how branches, reviews, merges, releases, and hotfixes are managed by a team.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: A trunk-based team may use short-lived branches and frequent merges while protecting the main branch with CI checks.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: The best workflow depends on release cadence, team size, risk, and deployment practices.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nA trunk-based team may use short-lived branches and frequent merges while protecting the main branch with CI checks.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software delivery pipeline may use Git workflows when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a trunk-based team may use short-lived branches and frequent merges while protecting the main branch with CI checks. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Git workflows as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Git workflows.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
