    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nGit workflows is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. The best workflow depends on release cadence, team size, risk, and deployment practices. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what Git workflows gives you: A Git workflow defines how branches, reviews, merges, releases, and hotfixes are managed by a team.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: The best workflow depends on release cadence, team size, risk, and deployment practices.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: A trunk-based team may use short-lived branches and frequent merges while protecting the main branch with CI checks.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nA trunk-based team may use short-lived branches and frequent merges while protecting the main branch with CI checks.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software delivery pipeline may use Git workflows when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a trunk-based team may use short-lived branches and frequent merges while protecting the main branch with CI checks. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Git workflows as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Git workflows.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
