    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Build automation misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Build automation. Build automation turns source code into verified artifacts through repeatable commands and dependencies.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Build automation: Build automation turns source code into verified artifacts through repeatable commands and dependencies.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: A pipeline can run `npm ci`, compile the application, run tests, and publish a versioned artifact.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: A deterministic build improves reproducibility but depends on pinned dependencies and a controlled environment.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nA pipeline can run `npm ci`, compile the application, run tests, and publish a versioned artifact.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software delivery pipeline may use Build automation when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a pipeline can run `npm ci`, compile the application, run tests, and publish a versioned artifact. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
