    slug: "devops-what-are-the-main-trade-offs-of-iac-58",
    shortDescription: "Frequently asked DevOps interview question about IaC.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nIaC is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. IaC improves repeatability but introduces state, dependency, and change-management concerns. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what IaC gives you: Infrastructure as Code describes infrastructure declaratively or programmatically so it can be versioned, reviewed, and reproduced.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: IaC improves repeatability but introduces state, dependency, and change-management concerns.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: A Terraform configuration can define a VPC, subnets, and load balancer rather than requiring manual console changes.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nA Terraform configuration can define a VPC, subnets, and load balancer rather than requiring manual console changes.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software delivery pipeline may use IaC when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a Terraform configuration can define a VPC, subnets, and load balancer rather than requiring manual console changes. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating IaC as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with IaC.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
