    shortDescription: "Frequently asked DevOps interview question about Terraform.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nIt matters because terraform is an infrastructure-as-code tool that compares desired configuration with tracked state and provider APIs to plan and apply infrastructure changes. In practice, the example shows how that behavior is used: a team can plan a change to an AWS service, review the plan, and apply it from CI.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe question is looking for reasoning rather than another definition. Explain the problem the concept solves, then say what would make you choose a different approach.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State the problem or need.\n2. Explain how Terraform addresses it: Terraform is an infrastructure-as-code tool that compares desired configuration with tracked state and provider APIs to plan and apply infrastructure changes.\n3. Tie that benefit to the concrete situation: A team can plan a change to an AWS service, review the plan, and apply it from CI.\n4. State the condition that can make the approach a poor fit: Terraform state must be protected and managed carefully because it represents infrastructure relationships and may contain sensitive data.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nAnswer the “why” directly, then connect it to a real engineering constraint. I would give one concrete situation and close by saying what would make me choose another approach.\n\n**Example :**\nA team can plan a change to an AWS service, review the plan, and apply it from CI.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software delivery pipeline may use Terraform when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a team can plan a change to an AWS service, review the plan, and apply it from CI. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Relying on intuition instead of the actual query plan.\n- Ignoring row counts and data distribution.\n- Fixing one query while creating a write, memory, or locking problem elsewhere.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nDo not repeat the definition as your whole answer. Explain the problem, the practical value, and the condition that would change your decision.",
