    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe usual flow for Terraform is to start with the inputs or conditions, apply the behavior described here, and verify the resulting state. Terraform is an infrastructure-as-code tool that compares desired configuration with tracked state and provider APIs to plan and apply infrastructure changes.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the mechanism and can explain a sequence, not just the end result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Establish the starting condition.\n2. Apply the mechanism: Terraform is an infrastructure-as-code tool that compares desired configuration with tracked state and provider APIs to plan and apply infrastructure changes.\n3. Verify the result using the concrete case: A team can plan a change to an AWS service, review the plan, and apply it from CI.\n4. Watch the trade-off or failure condition: Terraform state must be protected and managed carefully because it represents infrastructure relationships and may contain sensitive data.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nWalk through the normal sequence in order. Explain what changes at each important step, then mention how you would verify the result and what you would inspect if the operation failed.\n\n**Example :**\nA team can plan a change to an AWS service, review the plan, and apply it from CI.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software delivery pipeline may use Terraform when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a team can plan a change to an AWS service, review the plan, and apply it from CI. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Relying on intuition instead of the actual query plan.\n- Ignoring row counts and data distribution.\n- Fixing one query while creating a write, memory, or locking problem elsewhere.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the steps. That is more useful than listing commands or buzzwords.",
