    slug: "devops-how-would-you-troubleshoot-a-problem-involving-terraform-67",
    shortDescription: "Frequently asked DevOps interview question about Terraform.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Terraform misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Terraform. Terraform is an infrastructure-as-code tool that compares desired configuration with tracked state and provider APIs to plan and apply infrastructure changes.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Terraform: Terraform is an infrastructure-as-code tool that compares desired configuration with tracked state and provider APIs to plan and apply infrastructure changes.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: A team can plan a change to an AWS service, review the plan, and apply it from CI.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: Terraform state must be protected and managed carefully because it represents infrastructure relationships and may contain sensitive data.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nA team can plan a change to an AWS service, review the plan, and apply it from CI.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software delivery pipeline may use Terraform when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a team can plan a change to an AWS service, review the plan, and apply it from CI. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Relying on intuition instead of the actual query plan.\n- Ignoring row counts and data distribution.\n- Fixing one query while creating a write, memory, or locking problem elsewhere.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Terraform misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Terraform. Terraform is an infrastructure-as-code tool that compares desired configuration with tracked state and provider APIs to plan and apply infrastructure changes.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Terraform: Terraform is an infrastructure-as-code tool that compares desired configuration with tracked state and provider APIs to plan and apply infrastructure changes.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: A team can plan a change to an AWS service, review the plan, and apply it from CI.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: Terraform state must be protected and managed carefully because it represents infrastructure relationships and may contain sensitive data.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nA team can plan a change to an AWS service, review the plan, and apply it from CI.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software delivery pipeline may use Terraform when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a team can plan a change to an AWS service, review the plan, and apply it from CI. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Relying on intuition instead of the actual query plan.\n- Ignoring row counts and data distribution.\n- Fixing one query while creating a write, memory, or locking problem elsewhere.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
