    shortDescription: "Frequently asked DevOps interview question about Terraform.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nTerraform is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. Terraform state must be protected and managed carefully because it represents infrastructure relationships and may contain sensitive data. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what Terraform gives you: Terraform is an infrastructure-as-code tool that compares desired configuration with tracked state and provider APIs to plan and apply infrastructure changes.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: Terraform state must be protected and managed carefully because it represents infrastructure relationships and may contain sensitive data.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: A team can plan a change to an AWS service, review the plan, and apply it from CI.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nA team can plan a change to an AWS service, review the plan, and apply it from CI.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software delivery pipeline may use Terraform when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a team can plan a change to an AWS service, review the plan, and apply it from CI. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Relying on intuition instead of the actual query plan.\n- Ignoring row counts and data distribution.\n- Fixing one query while creating a write, memory, or locking problem elsewhere.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
