    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nConfiguration management keeps application or host settings consistent across environments and over time.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Configuration management accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Configuration management keeps application or host settings consistent across environments and over time.\n2. Use a concrete case: An automation process can apply the same baseline packages and service settings to a fleet of Linux hosts.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Centralization helps consistency, but configuration drift and secrets still need operational controls.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Configuration management first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nAn automation process can apply the same baseline packages and service settings to a fleet of Linux hosts.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software delivery pipeline may use Configuration management when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, an automation process can apply the same baseline packages and service settings to a fleet of Linux hosts. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Configuration management as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Configuration management.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Configuration management, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
