    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nI would explain Configuration management in plain language first, then give the technical detail that distinguishes it from nearby concepts. The practical anchor is: An automation process can apply the same baseline packages and service settings to a fleet of Linux hosts.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can communicate the concept clearly at an interview pace and adjust the depth when the interviewer asks a follow-up.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Give a one-sentence definition: Configuration management keeps application or host settings consistent across environments and over time.\n2. Explain one important behavior or distinction.\n3. Use the example to make it concrete: An automation process can apply the same baseline packages and service settings to a fleet of Linux hosts.\n4. Finish with the limitation: Centralization helps consistency, but configuration drift and secrets still need operational controls.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would keep the first answer concise, then wait for the interviewer to signal whether they want internals. If they go deeper, I would expand the mechanism and trade-offs rather than repeating the definition.\n\n**Example :**\nAn automation process can apply the same baseline packages and service settings to a fleet of Linux hosts.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software delivery pipeline may use Configuration management when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, an automation process can apply the same baseline packages and service settings to a fleet of Linux hosts. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Configuration management as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Configuration management.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nPractice saying {c} aloud in your own words. A technically correct answer is weaker if you cannot explain it without reading a script.",
