    slug: "devops-what-is-docker-81",
    shortDescription: "Frequently asked DevOps interview question about Docker.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nDocker packages an application and its dependencies into a container image that can run consistently across compatible container environments.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Docker accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Docker packages an application and its dependencies into a container image that can run consistently across compatible container environments.\n2. Use a concrete case: Package a service and its runtime dependencies into a Docker image so the same artifact can be tested in CI and deployed consistently.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Containers isolate processes but do not remove the need for host, network, storage, and image security.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Docker first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nPackage a service and its runtime dependencies into a Docker image so the same artifact can be tested in CI and deployed consistently.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software delivery pipeline may use Docker when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, package a service and its runtime dependencies into a Docker image so the same artifact can be tested in CI and deployed consistently. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Docker as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Docker.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Docker, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
