    shortDescription: "Frequently asked DevOps interview question about Docker.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe most common mistakes with Docker are usually misunderstandings of its actual behavior, poor handling of its constraints, or using it where a simpler approach would be enough. The important cases are below.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you have enough practical experience to recognize failure patterns rather than only describe the happy path.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Know the intended behavior: Docker packages an application and its dependencies into a container image that can run consistently across compatible container environments.\n2. Watch for these common failure patterns:\n- Treating Docker as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Docker.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n3. Use the example to check whether the implementation really behaves as expected: Package a service and its runtime dependencies into a Docker image so the same artifact can be tested in CI and deployed consistently.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would name two or three mistakes that are realistic for this concept and briefly explain why each one matters. I would finish with the one mistake I see most often in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nPackage a service and its runtime dependencies into a Docker image so the same artifact can be tested in CI and deployed consistently.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software delivery pipeline may use Docker when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, package a service and its runtime dependencies into a Docker image so the same artifact can be tested in CI and deployed consistently. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Docker as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Docker.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nChoose mistakes that are specific to Docker. Avoid generic answers such as “not testing enough” unless you explain exactly what can go wrong.",
