    slug: "devops-how-would-you-troubleshoot-a-problem-involving-docker-87",
    shortDescription: "Frequently asked DevOps interview question about Docker.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Docker misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Docker. Docker packages an application and its dependencies into a container image that can run consistently across compatible container environments.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Docker: Docker packages an application and its dependencies into a container image that can run consistently across compatible container environments.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: Package a service and its runtime dependencies into a Docker image so the same artifact can be tested in CI and deployed consistently.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: Containers isolate processes but do not remove the need for host, network, storage, and image security.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nPackage a service and its runtime dependencies into a Docker image so the same artifact can be tested in CI and deployed consistently.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software delivery pipeline may use Docker when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, package a service and its runtime dependencies into a Docker image so the same artifact can be tested in CI and deployed consistently. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Docker as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Docker.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
