    shortDescription: "Frequently asked DevOps interview question about Docker.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nDocker is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. Containers isolate processes but do not remove the need for host, network, storage, and image security. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what Docker gives you: Docker packages an application and its dependencies into a container image that can run consistently across compatible container environments.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: Containers isolate processes but do not remove the need for host, network, storage, and image security.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: Package a service and its runtime dependencies into a Docker image so the same artifact can be tested in CI and deployed consistently.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nPackage a service and its runtime dependencies into a Docker image so the same artifact can be tested in CI and deployed consistently.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software delivery pipeline may use Docker when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, package a service and its runtime dependencies into a Docker image so the same artifact can be tested in CI and deployed consistently. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Docker as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Docker.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
