    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nContainer security covers image provenance, dependency vulnerabilities, runtime privileges, secrets, network exposure, and host/container boundaries.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Container security accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Container security covers image provenance, dependency vulnerabilities, runtime privileges, secrets, network exposure, and host/container boundaries.\n2. Use a concrete case: A deployment can use a minimal trusted image, run as a non-root user, scan dependencies, and limit capabilities.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Security controls add build and operational work, but are preferable to relying on the image itself as a trust boundary.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Container security first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA deployment can use a minimal trusted image, run as a non-root user, scan dependencies, and limit capabilities.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software delivery pipeline may use Container security when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a deployment can use a minimal trusted image, run as a non-root user, scan dependencies, and limit capabilities. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating internal location as proof of trust.\n- Logging secrets or sensitive data while trying to improve diagnosis.\n- Checking authentication but forgetting authorization at the resource boundary.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Container security, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
