    shortDescription: "Frequently asked DevOps interview question about Container security.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nIt matters because container security covers image provenance, dependency vulnerabilities, runtime privileges, secrets, network exposure, and host/container boundaries. In practice, the example shows how that behavior is used: a deployment can use a minimal trusted image, run as a non-root user, scan dependencies, and limit capabilities.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe question is looking for reasoning rather than another definition. Explain the problem the concept solves, then say what would make you choose a different approach.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State the problem or need.\n2. Explain how Container security addresses it: Container security covers image provenance, dependency vulnerabilities, runtime privileges, secrets, network exposure, and host/container boundaries.\n3. Tie that benefit to the concrete situation: A deployment can use a minimal trusted image, run as a non-root user, scan dependencies, and limit capabilities.\n4. State the condition that can make the approach a poor fit: Security controls add build and operational work, but are preferable to relying on the image itself as a trust boundary.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nAnswer the “why” directly, then connect it to a real engineering constraint. I would give one concrete situation and close by saying what would make me choose another approach.\n\n**Example :**\nA deployment can use a minimal trusted image, run as a non-root user, scan dependencies, and limit capabilities.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software delivery pipeline may use Container security when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a deployment can use a minimal trusted image, run as a non-root user, scan dependencies, and limit capabilities. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating internal location as proof of trust.\n- Logging secrets or sensitive data while trying to improve diagnosis.\n- Checking authentication but forgetting authorization at the resource boundary.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nDo not repeat the definition as your whole answer. Explain the problem, the practical value, and the condition that would change your decision.",
