    shortDescription: "Frequently asked DevOps interview question about Kubernetes.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nKubernetes orchestrates containerized workloads using declarative resources for scheduling, networking, scaling, and lifecycle management.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Kubernetes accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Kubernetes orchestrates containerized workloads using declarative resources for scheduling, networking, scaling, and lifecycle management.\n2. Use a concrete case: A Deployment can maintain three replicas of an API and replace unhealthy Pods according to the desired state.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Kubernetes provides powerful automation but adds control-plane, networking, storage, and operational complexity.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Kubernetes first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA Deployment can maintain three replicas of an API and replace unhealthy Pods according to the desired state.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software delivery pipeline may use Kubernetes when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a Deployment can maintain three replicas of an API and replace unhealthy Pods according to the desired state. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Kubernetes as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Kubernetes.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Kubernetes, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
