    slug: "devops-how-would-you-troubleshoot-a-problem-involving-kubernetes-117",
    shortDescription: "Frequently asked DevOps interview question about Kubernetes.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Kubernetes misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Kubernetes. Kubernetes orchestrates containerized workloads using declarative resources for scheduling, networking, scaling, and lifecycle management.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Kubernetes: Kubernetes orchestrates containerized workloads using declarative resources for scheduling, networking, scaling, and lifecycle management.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: A Deployment can maintain three replicas of an API and replace unhealthy Pods according to the desired state.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: Kubernetes provides powerful automation but adds control-plane, networking, storage, and operational complexity.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nA Deployment can maintain three replicas of an API and replace unhealthy Pods according to the desired state.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software delivery pipeline may use Kubernetes when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a Deployment can maintain three replicas of an API and replace unhealthy Pods according to the desired state. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Kubernetes as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Kubernetes.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
