    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA Kubernetes Deployment manages a set of Pods and supports declarative updates and rollout history for the associated ReplicaSets.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Deployments accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: A Kubernetes Deployment manages a set of Pods and supports declarative updates and rollout history for the associated ReplicaSets.\n2. Use a concrete case: Use a repeatable deployment process that verifies the artifact, applies configuration safely, performs health checks, and supports rollback.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Rollouts need health checks and resource settings; a Deployment is not a substitute for application-level readiness.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Deployments first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nUse a repeatable deployment process that verifies the artifact, applies configuration safely, performs health checks, and supports rollback.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software delivery pipeline may use Deployments when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, use a repeatable deployment process that verifies the artifact, applies configuration safely, performs health checks, and supports rollback. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Deployments as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Deployments.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Deployments, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
