    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Deployments misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Deployments. A Kubernetes Deployment manages a set of Pods and supports declarative updates and rollout history for the associated ReplicaSets.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Deployments: A Kubernetes Deployment manages a set of Pods and supports declarative updates and rollout history for the associated ReplicaSets.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: Use a repeatable deployment process that verifies the artifact, applies configuration safely, performs health checks, and supports rollback.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: Rollouts need health checks and resource settings; a Deployment is not a substitute for application-level readiness.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nUse a repeatable deployment process that verifies the artifact, applies configuration safely, performs health checks, and supports rollback.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software delivery pipeline may use Deployments when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, use a repeatable deployment process that verifies the artifact, applies configuration safely, performs health checks, and supports rollback. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Deployments as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Deployments.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
