    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA Kubernetes Service gives a stable network identity for a set of Pods selected by labels.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Services accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: A Kubernetes Service gives a stable network identity for a set of Pods selected by labels.\n2. Use a concrete case: An API Service can route traffic to changing Pod IPs while clients continue using one stable service name.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: A Service handles network reachability, not application health semantics beyond configured endpoint behavior.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Services first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nAn API Service can route traffic to changing Pod IPs while clients continue using one stable service name.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software delivery pipeline may use Services when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, an API Service can route traffic to changing Pod IPs while clients continue using one stable service name. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Services as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Services.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Services, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
