    shortDescription: "Frequently asked DevOps interview question about Ingress.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nKubernetes Ingress defines HTTP or HTTPS routing from outside the cluster to services, typically through an ingress controller or gateway implementation.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Ingress accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Kubernetes Ingress defines HTTP or HTTPS routing from outside the cluster to services, typically through an ingress controller or gateway implementation.\n2. Use a concrete case: Host and path rules can route `api.example.com/orders` to an orders Service.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Ingress behavior depends on the controller; the Ingress resource itself does not implement all networking behavior.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Ingress first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nHost and path rules can route `api.example.com/orders` to an orders Service.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software delivery pipeline may use Ingress when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, host and path rules can route `api.example.com/orders` to an orders Service. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Ingress as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Ingress.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Ingress, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
