    question: "How would you troubleshoot a problem involving Ingress?",
    slug: "devops-how-would-you-troubleshoot-a-problem-involving-ingress-147",
    shortDescription: "Frequently asked DevOps interview question about Ingress.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Ingress misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Ingress. Kubernetes Ingress defines HTTP or HTTPS routing from outside the cluster to services, typically through an ingress controller or gateway implementation.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Ingress: Kubernetes Ingress defines HTTP or HTTPS routing from outside the cluster to services, typically through an ingress controller or gateway implementation.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: Host and path rules can route `api.example.com/orders` to an orders Service.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: Ingress behavior depends on the controller; the Ingress resource itself does not implement all networking behavior.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nHost and path rules can route `api.example.com/orders` to an orders Service.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software delivery pipeline may use Ingress when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, host and path rules can route `api.example.com/orders` to an orders Service. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Ingress as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Ingress.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Ingress misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Ingress. Kubernetes Ingress defines HTTP or HTTPS routing from outside the cluster to services, typically through an ingress controller or gateway implementation.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Ingress: Kubernetes Ingress defines HTTP or HTTPS routing from outside the cluster to services, typically through an ingress controller or gateway implementation.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: Host and path rules can route `api.example.com/orders` to an orders Service.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: Ingress behavior depends on the controller; the Ingress resource itself does not implement all networking behavior.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nHost and path rules can route `api.example.com/orders` to an orders Service.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software delivery pipeline may use Ingress when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, host and path rules can route `api.example.com/orders` to an orders Service. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Ingress as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Ingress.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
