    slug: "devops-what-are-the-main-trade-offs-of-ingress-148",
    shortDescription: "Frequently asked DevOps interview question about Ingress.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nIngress is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. Ingress behavior depends on the controller; the Ingress resource itself does not implement all networking behavior. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what Ingress gives you: Kubernetes Ingress defines HTTP or HTTPS routing from outside the cluster to services, typically through an ingress controller or gateway implementation.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: Ingress behavior depends on the controller; the Ingress resource itself does not implement all networking behavior.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: Host and path rules can route `api.example.com/orders` to an orders Service.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nHost and path rules can route `api.example.com/orders` to an orders Service.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software delivery pipeline may use Ingress when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, host and path rules can route `api.example.com/orders` to an orders Service. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Ingress as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Ingress.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
