    shortDescription: "Frequently asked DevOps interview question about Monitoring.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nMonitoring collects measurements and signals that show whether systems are healthy and meeting expected behavior.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Monitoring accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Monitoring collects measurements and signals that show whether systems are healthy and meeting expected behavior.\n2. Use a concrete case: A service can track request rate, error rate, latency, resource saturation, and dependency failures.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: More metrics do not automatically improve diagnosis; signals need useful thresholds and ownership.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Monitoring first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA service can track request rate, error rate, latency, resource saturation, and dependency failures.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software delivery pipeline may use Monitoring when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a service can track request rate, error rate, latency, resource saturation, and dependency failures. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Monitoring as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Monitoring.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Monitoring, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
