    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “What would you change about Monitoring when the system grows or an important assumption stops being true?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to Monitoring.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: Monitoring collects measurements and signals that show whether systems are healthy and meeting expected behavior.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: A service can track request rate, error rate, latency, resource saturation, and dependency failures.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: More metrics do not automatically improve diagnosis; signals need useful thresholds and ownership.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nA service can track request rate, error rate, latency, resource saturation, and dependency failures.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software delivery pipeline may use Monitoring when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a service can track request rate, error rate, latency, resource saturation, and dependency failures. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Monitoring as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Monitoring.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
