    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nLogging records structured or textual events that help explain application and system behavior.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Logging accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Logging records structured or textual events that help explain application and system behavior.\n2. Use a concrete case: When an order fails, structured logs can include an order ID and operation name so the request can be traced without logging secrets or full payment details.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Verbose logs increase cost and can expose sensitive data; structure and retention matter.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Logging first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nWhen an order fails, structured logs can include an order ID and operation name so the request can be traced without logging secrets or full payment details.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software delivery pipeline may use Logging when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, when an order fails, structured logs can include an order ID and operation name so the request can be traced without logging secrets or full payment details. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Logging as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Logging.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Logging, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
