    question: "What are the main trade-offs of Observability?",
    slug: "devops-what-are-the-main-trade-offs-of-observability-188",
    shortDescription: "Frequently asked DevOps interview question about Observability.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nObservability is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. Good observability requires consistent instrumentation and useful context, not merely collecting large volumes of telemetry. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what Observability gives you: Observability is the ability to infer internal system state from external signals, commonly including logs, metrics, and traces.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: Good observability requires consistent instrumentation and useful context, not merely collecting large volumes of telemetry.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: Use test failures together with logs, traces, and metrics to distinguish a product defect from a test-environment or infrastructure failure.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nUse test failures together with logs, traces, and metrics to distinguish a product defect from a test-environment or infrastructure failure.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software delivery pipeline may use Observability when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, use test failures together with logs, traces, and metrics to distinguish a product defect from a test-environment or infrastructure failure. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Observability as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Observability.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
