    shortDescription: "Frequently asked DevOps interview question about Release strategies.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nRelease strategies control how a new version reaches users, such as rolling, blue-green, or canary deployment.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Release strategies accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Release strategies control how a new version reaches users, such as rolling, blue-green, or canary deployment.\n2. Use a concrete case: A canary can expose a new version to a small percentage of traffic and expand it after health metrics remain within agreed limits.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: More controlled rollouts reduce blast radius but require routing, measurement, and rollback capability.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Release strategies first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA canary can expose a new version to a small percentage of traffic and expand it after health metrics remain within agreed limits.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software delivery pipeline may use Release strategies when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a canary can expose a new version to a small percentage of traffic and expand it after health metrics remain within agreed limits. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Release strategies as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Release strategies.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Release strategies, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
