    shortDescription: "Frequently asked GenerativeAI interview question about LLMs.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe main value of LLMs comes from the behavior it provides: A large language model predicts or generates token sequences from context learned during training and fine-tuning. It can be useful when that behavior reduces a specific engineering or business problem, as in this example: A chat application sends a sequence of messages and receives a generated continuation or structured response.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can distinguish a genuine benefit from a feature list and understand what the benefit costs.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Identify the behavior being provided: A large language model predicts or generates token sequences from context learned during training and fine-tuning.\n2. Connect it to the benefit shown by the example: A chat application sends a sequence of messages and receives a generated continuation or structured response.\n3. State the trade-off: Larger or more capable models can improve quality but often increase latency and inference cost.\n4. Avoid claiming a benefit that is not supported by the actual workload.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would name the practical benefits first, tie each one to the behavior, and then give the main trade-off. That shows I understand why the feature is valuable rather than simply listing advantages.\n\n**Example :**\nA chat application sends a sequence of messages and receives a generated continuation or structured response.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn ai-powered application may use LLMs when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a chat application sends a sequence of messages and receives a generated continuation or structured response. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating LLMs as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with LLMs.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain why the benefit matters for the workload. A benefit without a condition is usually too broad for a strong engineering answer.",
